---

<LessonPage page={page}>
<main class="wrap" data-core-duration="60–70 minutes" data-extension-duration="15–20 minutes">
  <p class="kicker"><span class="dot"></span> Vocabulary · Lesson {page.sequence}</p>
  <h1>Relationships: <em>describe the behavior before choosing the label</em></h1>
  <p class="standfirst">Relationship words carry assumptions about connection, trust, conflict, and responsibility. Precise English starts with observable context: <span class="ex">How do the people know each other?</span> <span class="ex">What changed?</span> <span class="ex">What action created support or harm?</span> <span class="ex">What is being requested?</span> <span class="ex">What limit will the speaker follow?</span> A label such as <span class="ex">close, supportive, difficult</span>, or <span class="ex">unreliable</span> becomes clearer when the listener can recover the behavior behind it.</p>
  <div class="meta-row">
    <span class="tag">60–70 min core</span>
    <span class="tag">connection · contact · support · trust</span>
    <span class="tag">conflict · request · boundary · repair</span>
    <span class="tag">15–20 min listening and retrieval</span>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-flow" aria-label="Lesson flow">
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Notice</b><span>Separate an event, its impact, a request, a boundary, and follow-through.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Distinguish</b><span>Name relationship type, contact change, support, trust, and conflict precisely.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Build</b><span>Use natural U.S. chunks for disagreement, apology, requests, and limits.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Use</b><span>Rehearse a fictional repair without forcing disclosure or reconciliation.</span></div>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line"><b>Can-do:</b> By the end, you can distinguish an acquaintance, coworker, close friend, neighbor, and clearly identified kind of partner; describe meeting, getting to know someone, keeping or losing touch, getting along, and growing apart; support claims about reliability and trust with actions; use natural U.S. expressions for disagreement, arguments, a falling-out, apology, and repair; distinguish an observation, impact, need, request, boundary, and agreement; state a limit through your own availability or action rather than trying to control another person; discuss a fictional conflict without requiring forgiveness or renewed contact; and make relationship stress and connected speech intelligible.</p>

  <h2>Two-minute retrieval</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You supported reliable with evidence, recovered used to for a changed routine, and selected reassured from the supplied supportive action.">
    <h3>Reactivate personality evidence, a changed past routine, and feeling</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Relationship foundation choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="reliable because she kept every promise">reliable because she kept every promise</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="used to meet">used to meet</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="reassured">reassured</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="perfect without evidence">perfect without evidence</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The project record shows that Lina completed every promised task on time. The evidence-based description is “Lina was <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="reliable because she kept every promise" data-hint="Choose the personality claim that includes the supplied behavioral evidence." data-fix="Lina was reliable because she kept every promise.">choose</button>.”</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The weekly routine ended after Maya moved: “Maya and Lina <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="used to meet" data-hint="Use used to plus the base verb for a former repeated routine." data-fix="Maya and Lina used to meet every Friday.">choose</button> every Friday.”</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Before Lina’s presentation, Maya listened and reminded her that she was prepared. Lina felt <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="reassured" data-hint="Choose the feeling of reduced worry after supportive words." data-fix="Lina felt reassured after Maya listened and encouraged her.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Notice an event, impact, request, boundary, and repair</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Fictional Cedar House project conversation</h3>
    <p><b>Nora:</b> <span class="ex">“When the schedule changed and no message arrived, I had to prepare the room alone. I felt unprepared and frustrated.”</span></p>
    <p><b>Nora:</b> <span class="ex">“Could you send an update by 3:00 when a deadline is at risk? I do not answer project messages after 8:00 p.m.; I will reply the next morning.”</span></p>
    <p><b>Eli:</b> <span class="ex">“You needed an earlier warning, and I did not send one. I should have told you. I will send the 3:00 update.”</span></p>
    <p><b>Later:</b> Eli sent every promised update for the next three weeks. Nora said that the consistent action was beginning to rebuild trust.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>What observable event affected Nora?</li>
      <li>Which sentence asks Eli to do something?</li>
      <li>Which sentence states Nora’s own availability and next action?</li>
      <li>What evidence supports the later trust statement?</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You separated the missing update, the 3:00 request, Nora’s after-8:00 boundary, and Eli’s repeated follow-through.">
    <h3>Discovery check: what communication job does each detail perform?</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Relationship conversation choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="no message arrived after the schedule changed">no message arrived after the schedule changed</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="send an update by 3:00">send an update by 3:00</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="reply the next morning to messages sent after 8:00 p.m.">reply the next morning to messages sent after 8:00 p.m.</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="sent every promised update for three weeks">sent every promised update for three weeks</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The observable event was that <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="no message arrived after the schedule changed" data-hint="Choose the specific event, not a broad personality label." data-fix="No message arrived after the schedule changed.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Nora’s request asks Eli to <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="send an update by 3:00" data-hint="A request asks another person for a specific action." data-fix="Nora asks Eli to send an update by 3:00 when a deadline is at risk.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Nora’s boundary says she will <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="reply the next morning to messages sent after 8:00 p.m." data-hint="The boundary describes Nora’s availability and what she will do." data-fix="Nora will reply the next morning to project messages sent after 8:00 p.m.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The later evidence of follow-through is that Eli <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="sent every promised update for three weeks" data-hint="Choose the repeated action after the conversation, not the apology alone." data-fix="Eli sent every promised update for the next three weeks.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Name the relationship from the supplied context, not an assumption</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>word</th><th>core relationship</th><th>what the word does not prove</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">acquaintance</span></td><td>someone you know, but not closely</td><td>how often you meet or whether you like each other</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">coworker</span></td><td>someone who works in the same workplace</td><td>friendship outside work</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">close friend</span></td><td>a friend described with closeness, trust, or meaningful shared knowledge</td><td>one universal amount of contact or one cultural behavior</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">neighbor</span></td><td>someone who lives nearby</td><td>friendship, agreement, or frequent contact</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">partner</span></td><td>a person joined in a clearly understood romantic, business, project, or activity relationship</td><td>which kind of relationship without context</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p><span class="ex">Colleague</span> can refer to a coworker or another person in the same profession or field, so it may be broader than <span class="ex">coworker</span>. <span class="ex">Partner</span> is gender-neutral but still needs enough context when the type matters: <span class="ex">romantic partner, business partner, project partner</span>. Relationship norms vary across people, communities, and cultures. Eye contact, directness, physical affection, and frequency of messages do not prove closeness or respect by themselves.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You used the stated degree of familiarity, shared workplace, close friendship evidence, nearby home, and business agreement rather than guessing another relationship.">
    <h3>Choose the relationship label the sentence actually establishes</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Relationship label choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="acquaintance">acquaintance</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="coworker">coworker</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="close friend">close friend</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="neighbor">neighbor</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="business partner">business partner</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Omar recognizes Priya from two community meetings, but they have never spoken privately or spent time together. Priya is currently an <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="acquaintance" data-hint="The context establishes recognition and limited familiarity, not closeness." data-fix="Priya is currently an acquaintance of Omar’s.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Jules and Ren work for the same company at the same office. The sentence establishes that Ren is Jules’s <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="coworker" data-hint="Choose the U.S. word for someone in the same workplace." data-fix="Ren is Jules’s coworker.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>For twelve years, Ana and Mei have shared important experiences, supported each other through difficult periods, and trusted each other with private concerns. The context supports <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="close friend" data-hint="The sentence explicitly supplies long-term closeness, trust, and mutual support." data-fix="Mei is a close friend of Ana’s.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>Sam lives in apartment 4A, and Tori lives next door in 4B. The only relationship established is that Sam is Tori’s <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="neighbor" data-hint="Nearby homes establish a neighbor relationship, not necessarily friendship." data-fix="Sam is Tori’s neighbor.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>Dev and Luis signed an agreement to own and operate a design company together. Luis is Dev’s <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="business partner" data-hint="The supplied agreement identifies the kind of partnership." data-fix="Luis is Dev’s business partner.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Track contact and closeness as separate changes</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p><span class="ex">Meet</span> names a first encounter or planned gathering. <span class="ex">Get to know someone</span> is the gradual process of learning about the person. <span class="ex">Get along with someone</span> means the relationship is generally friendly or works reasonably well; it does not mean the people agree about everything. <span class="ex">Keep in touch</span> means continue contact; <span class="ex">lose touch</span> means contact stops. <span class="ex">Grow apart</span> or <span class="ex">drift apart</span> means become less close over time and does not automatically imply an argument.</p>
  <p><span class="ex">Have something in common</span> names a shared interest, experience, background, or characteristic. It does not prove closeness. Avoid converting message frequency into a universal measure of care: some close relationships involve frequent contact, while others do not.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You distinguished gradual familiarity, generally friendly interaction, continued contact, stopped contact, and reduced closeness without inventing conflict.">
    <h3>Choose the process from the stated contact history</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Relationship development choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="got to know">got to know</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="get along">get along</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="keep in touch">keep in touch</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="lost touch">lost touch</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="grew apart">grew apart</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>During six months on the same volunteer team, Noor and Val gradually learned about each other’s interests and working styles. They <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="got to know" data-hint="Choose the process of becoming familiar over time." data-fix="Noor and Val got to know each other on the volunteer team.">choose</button> each other.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Jae and Morgan sometimes disagree about design, but they communicate respectfully and work well together. They generally <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="get along" data-hint="The relationship works well even though agreement is not complete." data-fix="Jae and Morgan generally get along.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>After moving to different cities, Inez and Rosa still call each other every month. They <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="keep in touch" data-hint="Choose the expression for maintaining contact across distance." data-fix="Inez and Rosa keep in touch by calling every month.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>After the course ended, Arun and Lee changed phone numbers and stopped communicating. They <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="lost touch" data-hint="The context says contact stopped, without stating a conflict." data-fix="Arun and Lee lost touch after the course.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>There was no argument, but over several years Kim and Sol developed different routines and became less close. They <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="grew apart" data-hint="Choose the gradual reduction in closeness without an implied fight." data-fix="Kim and Sol grew apart over several years.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Support claims about trust with a specific action</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p><span class="ex">Rely on someone</span> means depend on that person for something stated or understood. <span class="ex">Be there for someone</span> is an informal expression for giving support when needed. <span class="ex">Follow through</span> means do the action you said you would do. <span class="ex">Let someone down</span> means fail to provide expected support or fail to do what was promised. <span class="ex">Rebuild trust</span> describes a process after trust has been damaged; one apology may begin repair but does not guarantee restored trust.</p>
  <p>Support does not require agreement with every choice. Trust can differ by domain: a person may be trusted with a confidential story but not with a deadline, or the reverse. Use the behavior and situation rather than a permanent label whenever the distinction matters.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You named dependable help, emotional presence, completed commitment, failed expectation, and a gradual trust-repair process from the stated behavior.">
    <h3>Choose the trust or support relationship the evidence establishes</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Trust and support choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="rely on">rely on</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="was there for">was there for</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="followed through">followed through</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="let Nora down">let Nora down</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="rebuild trust">rebuild trust</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Whenever the monthly figures are due, Ava checks them accurately by the agreed time. The team can <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="rely on" data-hint="Choose the phrase for depending on Ava for the stated task." data-fix="The team can rely on Ava to check the monthly figures accurately and on time.">choose</button> Ava for this task.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>After Theo received disappointing news, Kai listened, stayed with him, and helped with the immediate practical tasks. Kai <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="was there for" data-hint="Choose the informal support expression supported by listening and practical help." data-fix="Kai was there for Theo after the disappointing news.">choose</button> Theo.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Mina promised to send the accessibility notes by noon and sent them at 11:45. She <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="followed through" data-hint="The promised action was completed as agreed." data-fix="Mina followed through on her promise to send the notes by noon.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>Eli promised to warn Nora about schedule risks, but he knew about the delay and sent no warning. In that specific commitment, he <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="let Nora down" data-hint="Choose the phrase for failing to provide the expected promised support." data-fix="Eli let Nora down by not warning her about the known delay.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>After missing the warning, Eli apologized and then sent every agreed update for three weeks. The repeated action could help <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="rebuild trust" data-hint="The context describes a gradual process after trust was damaged." data-fix="Eli’s repeated follow-through could help rebuild trust.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Use natural U.S. chunks for conflict and repair</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>relationship</th><th>natural pattern</th><th>example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>different view</td><td><span class="ex">disagree with a person about an issue</span></td><td><span class="ex">I disagree with Eli about the schedule.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>heated conflict</td><td><span class="ex">argue with a person about an issue</span></td><td><span class="ex">They argued about the deadline.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>serious break</td><td><span class="ex">have a falling-out with someone</span></td><td><span class="ex">They had a falling-out last year.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>acknowledge harm</td><td><span class="ex">apologize to a person for an action</span></td><td><span class="ex">Eli apologized to Nora for missing the update.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>examine together</td><td><span class="ex">discuss an issue / talk an issue through</span></td><td><span class="ex">They discussed the delay and talked the plan through.</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p><span class="ex">Had a falling-out</span> is especially natural in U.S. English for a serious disagreement that damages a relationship. <span class="ex">Fell out with</span> is also understandable and used across English varieties. <span class="ex">Make up</span> can mean reconcile after a conflict, but context must separate it from <span class="ex">invent a story</span>, <span class="ex">constitute</span>, and cosmetic <span class="ex">makeup</span>.</p>
  <p>An apology does not force forgiveness, restored trust, renewed contact, or agreement. <span class="ex">Talk it through</span> may create understanding, but not every conflict is resolved, and reconciliation is not always wanted or appropriate. Describe the outcome the context actually supplies.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You used disagree with and about, argue about, the U.S.-natural falling-out noun, apologize to and for, and discuss without an extra preposition.">
    <h3>Choose the complete conflict or repair relationship</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Conflict and repair choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="disagree with Omar about">disagree with Omar about</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="argued about">argued about</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="had a falling-out">had a falling-out</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="apologized to Nora for">apologized to Nora for</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="discussed">discussed</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>I respect Omar, but our views on the start time differ: “I <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="disagree with Omar about" data-hint="Use disagree with before the person and about before the issue." data-fix="I disagree with Omar about the start time.">choose</button> the start time.”</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The voices became heated during a conflict over the budget: “They <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="argued about" data-hint="Use argue about before the issue when the people are already the subject." data-fix="They argued about the budget.">choose</button> the budget.”</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>After a serious disagreement last year, Cam and Jo stopped speaking for six months. In natural U.S. English, they <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="had a falling-out" data-hint="Choose the noun phrase for a serious disagreement that damaged the relationship." data-fix="Cam and Jo had a falling-out last year.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>Eli addressed the person and named his action: “Eli <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="apologized to Nora for" data-hint="Apologize to introduces the person; for introduces the action." data-fix="Eli apologized to Nora for missing the update.">choose</button> missing the update.”</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>No preposition follows discuss before its object: “Nora and Eli <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="discussed" data-hint="Discuss takes the issue directly; talk uses about." data-fix="Nora and Eli discussed the communication problem.">choose</button> the communication problem.”</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Separate an observation, impact, need, request, boundary, and agreement</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>A constructive account can move through several jobs without pretending one formula guarantees a good result:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><b>Observation:</b> name a specific event without adding an unsupported character judgment: <span class="ex">The update arrived after the client call.</span></li>
    <li><b>Impact or feeling:</b> state the effect on the speaker: <span class="ex">I had to answer alone, and I felt unprepared.</span></li>
    <li><b>Need:</b> name what matters in this situation: <span class="ex">I need earlier information when a deadline is at risk.</span></li>
    <li><b>Request:</b> ask another person for a specific action they can accept, decline, or negotiate: <span class="ex">Could you send an update by 3:00?</span></li>
    <li><b>Boundary:</b> state the speaker’s own limit and action: <span class="ex">I do not take project calls after 8:00; I reply the next morning.</span></li>
    <li><b>Agreement:</b> record a specific observable action, person, and time: <span class="ex">Eli will send a status message by 3:00 every Tuesday.</span></li>
  </ol>
  <p>Starting with <span class="ex">I feel</span> does not automatically remove blame: <span class="ex">I feel that you are selfish</span> still presents a judgment about the other person. <span class="ex">Always</span> and <span class="ex">never</span> can be literally accurate, but they often overgeneralize when the speaker has not checked every case. A boundary is not a special word that makes any demand respectful. State what you will do or what access you offer. A request allows a real response.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You identified the event, personal impact, underlying need, negotiable request, and self-directed boundary from the grammar and context.">
    <h3>Choose the communication job, not the sentence that sounds nicest</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Communication job choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="observation">observation</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="impact">impact</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="need">need</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="request">request</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="boundary">boundary</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>“The schedule changed at noon, and no update arrived before the 4:00 client call.” This sentence names the specific event, so it is the <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="observation" data-hint="Choose the job that reports the event without a character label." data-fix="The schedule change and missing update form the observation.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>“I had to answer the client alone, and I felt unprepared.” This sentence describes the effect on Nora, so it states the <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="impact" data-hint="Choose the job that names what happened to the speaker and how it felt." data-fix="Answering alone and feeling unprepared describe the impact on Nora.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>“I need earlier information when a deadline is at risk.” This sentence names what matters in the situation, so it states the <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="need" data-hint="The sentence does not yet ask Eli for one exact action." data-fix="Nora states a need for earlier information.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>“Could you send an update by 3:00?” This asks Eli for a specific action that he can answer, so it is a <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="request" data-hint="Choose the job that asks another person for an observable action." data-fix="Could you send an update by 3:00? is a request.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>“I do not answer project messages after 8:00; I reply the next morning.” This states Nora’s availability and action, so it is a <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="boundary" data-hint="Choose the job that states the speaker’s own limit and response." data-fix="Nora’s after-8:00 availability and next-morning response form a boundary.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Sound natural: Link the relationship and keep the contrast clear</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>Keep the stressed syllable clear in <span class="ex">ac-QUAIN-tance</span> /əˈkweɪntəns/, <span class="ex">SUP-port-ive</span> /səˈpɔrtɪv/, <span class="ex">BOUND-a-ry</span> /ˈbaʊndəri/, and <span class="ex">a-POL-o-gize</span> /əˈpɑlədʒaɪz/. <span class="ex">Coworker</span> begins with the main stress in common U.S. speech: /ˈkoʊˌwɝkər/.</p>
  <p>Link across word boundaries in <span class="ex">know_each_other</span>, <span class="ex">rely_on</span>, and <span class="ex">talk_it_through</span>. In neutral citation practice, the particle is often prominent in <span class="ex">get aLONG, grow aPART, make UP</span>, but real information focus can move the strongest stress: <span class="ex">We got along at <b>WORK</b>, not at home.</span> Clarity matters more than copying one accent.</p>
  <div class="quiz" data-quiz data-success-message="Correct. You heard contact without conflict, support backed by action, the falling-out noun, a request versus a boundary, and the stress contrast that changes with information focus.">
    <h3>Tutor-read listening: recover the relationship, not one keyword</h3>
    <p class="note-line"><b>Tutor:</b> Read each line once naturally, then repeat if requested. <b>Learner:</b> Choose the relationship or spoken contrast you heard.</p>
    <div class="quiz-list">
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="They became less close gradually, and no conflict is stated." data-hint="Listen for over the years and never had an argument." data-fix="They became less close gradually, and no conflict is stated."><p class="quiz-question">1. Tutor line: “They grew apart over the years, but they never had an argument.” What relationship change is stated?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="They became less close gradually, and no conflict is stated.">They became less close gradually, and no conflict is stated.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="They had a serious falling-out and stopped speaking immediately.">They had a serious falling-out and stopped speaking immediately.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="They became business partners through a contract.">They became business partners through a contract.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Kai supported Theo by listening and helping with immediate tasks." data-hint="Be there for is supported by the two actions in the line." data-fix="Kai supported Theo by listening and helping with immediate tasks."><p class="quiz-question">2. Tutor line: “Kai was there for Theo: he listened and helped with the immediate tasks.” What evidence supports the phrase?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Kai supported Theo by listening and helping with immediate tasks.">Kai supported Theo by listening and helping with immediate tasks.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Kai agreed with every decision Theo had ever made.">Kai agreed with every decision Theo had ever made.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Kai and Theo lived in neighboring apartments.">Kai and Theo lived in neighboring apartments.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="They had a serious disagreement that damaged the relationship." data-hint="A falling-out is a serious conflict, not simply a different opinion." data-fix="They had a serious disagreement that damaged the relationship."><p class="quiz-question">3. Tutor line: “Cam and Jo had a falling-out last year.” What does the noun phrase mean here?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="They had a serious disagreement that damaged the relationship.">They had a serious disagreement that damaged the relationship.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="They gradually learned about each other for the first time.">They gradually learned about each other for the first time.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="They fell physically from a high place.">They fell physically from a high place.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The first sentence asks Eli to act; the second states Nora’s own availability and response." data-hint="Could you asks another person; I do not and I reply state the speaker’s limit and action." data-fix="The first sentence asks Eli to act; the second states Nora’s own availability and response."><p class="quiz-question">4. Tutor lines: “Could you message me by three? I do not answer project messages after eight; I reply the next morning.” What distinction did you hear?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The first sentence asks Eli to act; the second states Nora’s own availability and response.">The first sentence asks Eli to act; the second states Nora’s own availability and response.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Both sentences diagnose Eli’s permanent personality.">Both sentences diagnose Eli’s permanent personality.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Both sentences force forgiveness after a conflict.">Both sentences force forgiveness after a conflict.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Work receives contrastive stress because the relationship differs by setting." data-hint="The correction contrasts work with home, so the setting word carries the focus." data-fix="Work receives contrastive stress because the relationship differs by setting."><p class="quiz-question">5. Tutor line: “We got along at WORK, not at home.” Why is <span class="ex">work</span> prominent?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Work receives contrastive stress because the relationship differs by setting.">Work receives contrastive stress because the relationship differs by setting.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Along must always be the only stressed word in every context.">Along must always be the only stressed word in every context.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Work is prominent because it is a boundary.">Work is prominent because it is a boundary.</button></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-quiz>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-quiz>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
