  <h2>Read repair as words plus later behavior</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Fictional Cedar House project record</h3>
    <p><b>Monday, 10:00 a.m.:</b> Nora and Eli agreed that Eli would send the room plan by Wednesday at 3:00 p.m.</p>
    <p><b>Wednesday, 2:30 p.m.:</b> Eli discovered missing measurements. He felt embarrassed about being behind and sent no message.</p>
    <p><b>Thursday, 9:00 a.m.:</b> The late plan arrived after Nora had already explained the delay to the client alone.</p>
    <p><b>Conversation:</b> Nora described the missing warning and its impact, requested a 3:00 risk update, and stated that she replies the next morning to project messages sent after 8:00 p.m. Eli summarized her need, apologized for not warning her, and said his embarrassment explained his silence but did not remove its impact.</p>
    <p><b>Agreement and evidence:</b> They agreed on a status message two days before every deadline and a risk update by 3:00 p.m. Eli sent every agreed message for the next three weeks. Nora said trust was beginning to rebuild; she did not say the earlier problem had disappeared.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz" data-quiz data-success-message="Correct. You identified the missed warning, explanation without erased responsibility, specific request, self-directed boundary, and repeated behavior supporting gradual trust repair.">
