  <h2>Notice and repair the relationship pattern</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="micro-drill" data-spot-error data-answer="know" data-hint="The state began in the past and continues now." data-fix="We’ve known each other since university." data-why="Use the Present Perfect for a state that began at the stated past starting point and continues now." data-success-message="Correct. Have known connects the university starting point to the present relationship.">
    <h3>Repair 1: connect the continuing state to now</h3><p>Tap the verb form that does not connect the past starting point to the present.</p><div class="spot-options"><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="we">We</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="know">know</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="each">each other</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="since">since university.</button></div><p class="hidden-answer" data-error-feedback></p>
  </div>
  <div class="micro-drill" data-spot-error data-answer="well" data-hint="The U.S. relationship phrase needs along before the manner word well." data-fix="I get along well with most of my coworkers." data-why="Get along with someone is the complete relationship phrase; well can then describe how the people get along." data-success-message="Correct. Get along well with now contains the full U.S. relationship chunk.">
    <h3>Repair 2: complete <span class="ex">get along with</span></h3><p>Tap the word before which the missing particle belongs.</p><div class="spot-options"><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="i">I</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="get">get</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="well">well</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="coworkers">with most of my coworkers.</button></div><p class="hidden-answer" data-error-feedback></p>
  </div>
  <div class="micro-drill" data-spot-error data-answer="about" data-hint="Discuss takes the topic directly as its object." data-fix="We need to discuss what happened. / We need to talk about what happened." data-why="Discuss takes a direct object without about; talk uses about before the topic." data-success-message="Correct. Discuss now takes what happened directly as its object.">
    <h3>Repair 3: choose <span class="ex">discuss</span> or <span class="ex">talk about</span></h3><p>Tap the extra preposition after <span class="ex">discuss</span>.</p><div class="spot-options"><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="we">We need</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="discuss">to discuss</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="about">about</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="what">what happened.</button></div><p class="hidden-answer" data-error-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Practice sensitive language without disclosing a real relationship</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p class="note-line">You never need to reveal a real family relationship, friendship, romantic relationship, workplace conflict, breakup, estrangement, betrayal, argument, boundary, trauma, abuse, health condition, private message, identity, or contact decision. This lesson teaches English, not counseling. If a scenario feels unsafe or too personal, skip it and use Nora, Eli, and the fictional Cedar House record. No task requires forgiveness, reconciliation, renewed contact, or remaining in a relationship.</p>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Relationship map:</b> Name only the relationship type and history that the fictional evidence establishes. Do not infer romance, friendship, or conflict from one behavior.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Behavior map:</b> Replace a broad label with the exact event, action, frequency, person affected, and result.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Conversation map:</b> Separate observation, impact, need, request, boundary, response, and agreement. The other speaker may accept, decline, or negotiate the request.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Repair map:</b> Distinguish apology, explanation, responsibility, changed behavior, trust repair, forgiveness, reconciliation, and continued contact. Do not claim an outcome the card does not supply.</div>

  <h2>Final production and check</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Final production: run a 90-second fictional repair conversation</h3>
    <p>Use Nora, Eli, and the Cedar House record. Speaker A names the exact event, impact, need, one negotiable request, and one self-directed boundary. Speaker B paraphrases the need, accepts the responsibility supported by the record, explains the silence without erasing its impact, apologizes to the person for the action, and proposes a specific agreement. The speakers may accept or modify the agreement. A narrator then identifies the later follow-through and describes trust as beginning to rebuild, not instantly restored. Use at least six target chunks and make the stressed syllables and linked relationship phrases intelligible.</p>
    <p><b>Evidence check:</b> Can the listener identify how the people know each other, what happened, who was affected, what support or failure occurred, which sentence is a request, which is a boundary, what responsibility was accepted, what agreement is observable, and what later behavior supports the limited trust claim?</p>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz" data-quiz data-success-message="Correct. You identified a relationship without assumptions, preserved contact without conflict, supported trust with behavior, used the U.S.-natural falling-out phrase, separated request from boundary, and kept repair distinct from forced reconciliation.">
    <h3>Final check: choose the relationship account the context supports</h3>
    <div class="quiz-list">
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="They work at the same office, so they are coworkers; the sentence does not establish friendship." data-hint="Use only the shared-workplace relationship supplied in the context." data-fix="They work at the same office, so they are coworkers; the sentence does not establish friendship."><p class="quiz-question">1. Two people share a workplace, but no personal connection is described. Which account is precise?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="They work at the same office, so they are coworkers; the sentence does not establish friendship.">They work at the same office, so they are coworkers; the sentence does not establish friendship.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="They are automatically close friends because every coworker relationship is personal.">They are automatically close friends because every coworker relationship is personal.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="They are romantic partners because they use the same building.">They are romantic partners because they use the same building.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="They grew apart over several years, and the context states that no argument occurred." data-hint="Reduced closeness does not automatically imply a falling-out." data-fix="They grew apart over several years, and the context states that no argument occurred."><p class="quiz-question">2. Which sentence accurately describes gradual reduced closeness without conflict?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="They grew apart over several years, and the context states that no argument occurred.">They grew apart over several years, and the context states that no argument occurred.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="They had a falling-out because all reduced contact proves a serious argument.">They had a falling-out because all reduced contact proves a serious argument.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="They kept in touch because they stopped every form of contact.">They kept in touch because they stopped every form of contact.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Eli’s apology begins repair; three weeks of follow-through provide later evidence that trust may be rebuilding." data-hint="Separate the apology words from the repeated later behavior." data-fix="Eli’s apology begins repair; three weeks of follow-through provide later evidence that trust may be rebuilding."><p class="quiz-question">3. Which account supports the trust claim with behavior?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Eli’s apology begins repair; three weeks of follow-through provide later evidence that trust may be rebuilding.">Eli’s apology begins repair; three weeks of follow-through provide later evidence that trust may be rebuilding.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="One apology instantly guarantees complete trust forever.">One apology instantly guarantees complete trust forever.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Repeated follow-through has no possible relationship to reliability.">Repeated follow-through has no possible relationship to reliability.</button></div></div>
